\title[The left-edge spike of a Fermat window is not one object]
{The Left-Edge Spike of a Fermat Window is Not One Object:\\
Exact Band Geometry of the Quadratic Residues $j^2-N$}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{26 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the residue sequence $v(j)=j^{2}-N$ along the Fermat scan window
$j\in(s,3s]$, $s=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$, with positions normalised as
$u=(j-s)/(2s)\in(0,1]$.  Empirical scans report a pronounced excess of smooth
residues at the left edge --- a ``spike'' carrying roughly $8.6\%$ of the recorded
mass in the first decile --- and it is natural to test whether that excess is a
magnitude artifact by discarding residues below the modulus scale.  We prove that
this test is \emph{provably degenerate}: for every $96$-bit modulus $N$ and every
first-decile position $j$ one has $v(j)<2^{95}$, so a ``$v\ge 2^{95}$'' filter
deletes $100\%$ of the first-decile population by arithmetic alone.  The mechanism
is scale free: for $N\ge 2^{16}$ a first-decile residue satisfies $100\,v(j)<45N$,
hence $2v(j)<N$, so the residue always loses a binary digit relative to the
modulus; both size hypotheses are load-bearing, with explicit counterexamples at
$N=36482$ and $N=962$.  Since $v$ is strictly increasing, every bit-length band is
a positional interval, the sub-$T$ population equals
$\min(3s,\lfloor\sqrt{N+T-1}\rfloor)-s$ exactly, and the band histogram telescopes
into differences of integer square roots --- a deterministic function of $N$.  For
$96$-bit moduli the excluded band occupies between $11\%$ and $21\%$ of the window.
In the continuum the transition is governed by the crossing curve
$u_{0}(N)=\bigl(\sqrt{1+2^{95}/N}-1\bigr)/2$, strictly decreasing in $N$ and
confined to $\bigl((\sqrt6-2)/4,(\sqrt2-1)/2\bigr]\subset(0.1123,0.2072]$; the
decile boundary $0.1$ lies strictly below the lower endpoint, which is the
structural cause of the degeneracy and reproduces the observed post-filter support
edge $u\approx 0.114$.  A discrete--continuum bridge shows the integer count differs
from the continuum prediction by at most $2$ positions, or $3/s$ in normalised
fraction.  Finally, explicit $96$-bit witnesses exhibit a full-size residue at
$u=0.15$ for one modulus and a sub-$2^{95}$ residue at $u=0.20$ for another, so
position and bit-length are genuinely two stratifications of the window across the
modulus ensemble.  Combined with a refit of the empirical data (edge weight
$0.0794\to 0.0403$, confidence interval excluding zero), the conclusion is that the
observed spike conflates a magnitude-driven inclusion channel with a residual,
genuine excess among full-size residues.
\end{abstract}

